Coreshell drives, but doesn't include, change or link with, the MiSTer-devel
cores and Main_MiSTer (Alexey Melnikov "Sorgelig" and the MiSTer devs, GPL),
odelot's RetroAchievements cores and MiSTer_RA, and every core Update All
installs: each under its own licence, by its own authors. Update All's
databases are fetched at run time and not included.

The shim Mains Coreshell can build (python3 -m coreshell mains build) are
GPL-3.0 forks of Main_MiSTer and of each fork (MiSTer_RA's with Phosphor's in-game link,
super-memcard main/link), credited in mains.json to their
authors (the MiSTer devs, odelot, Anime0t4ku, the Zaparoo Project, owenb321,
neofreno, kimchiman52). The shim is in super-memcard, under GPL-3.0; Coreshell
only runs git and make, and no GPL source is part of Coreshell.

corehost (codename Fushi Tensei, https://github.com/f3bandit/corehost) is a fork
of Main_MiSTer under GPL-3.0 or later, in its own repository. Coreshell starts
it and talks to it over its local socket (/tmp/corehost.sock), and the launcher
reads which Main is running; no corehost code is part of Coreshell, so
Coreshell stays under the Apache License 2.0. The same holds for the other parts
of Infinite Tsukuyomi (corehost's project), which keep their own licences.

dumps/ and systems/ are taken from each core: its menu as the core reports it,
including the core's own menu text (the "confstr" field, word for word) with its
option names, values and bit positions. That text belongs to each core's authors
under the core's own licence (most MiSTer cores are GPL); each file names the core
and build it came from. These files are data Coreshell reads, never compiled or
linked into it, and they are not covered by Coreshell's Apache License.

